# Validation cost
Use when checks are slow, CI repeats setup, caches are ineffective, or concurrent
local runs contend for resources. Diagnose with existing commands first. Modify
validation infrastructure only within the requested implementation scope.
## Measure the bottleneck
Measure feedback wall time separately from total runner/compute time. Record
queueing, checkout, install/build, fixture setup, and test execution where the
tools expose them. Find the dependency chain delaying completion and the slowest
shard. Faster completion can still consume more aggregate compute.
Compare like-for-like inputs, runner capacity, concurrency, and cache state.
Keep tail latency visible; do not infer a stable improvement from one fast run.
Report unknown costs as unmeasured and retain correctness checks while optimizing.
Before reporting a speedup or a regression, make the measuring harness print its
error count and a count of the work it actually did. A run that failed early or
measured nothing then cannot read as zero cost. Take the baseline first, on the
unchanged code, and report a median of repeated runs. Keep a change only when the
metric moves past the run-to-run noise with the gate still green; otherwise revert
the whole change, not part of it. When the feature does not exist on the base
branch, compare against an absolute budget instead of a ratio to the base.
Try remedies in order of cost: skip the work, do not repeat it, do less of
it, do it later, do it concurrently, and only then make it cheaper. Do not state a
performance ceiling you have not run.
## Reduce repeated setup before adding workers
Keep checks individually callable, and share preparation in aggregate execution.
Use maintained entry points with the same semantics locally and in CI. Group
short checks when runner startup and dependency setup dominate, preserving every
check's status and logs. Filter unnecessary work before allocating a runner where
the workflow supports it. Keep non-gating bookkeeping off the merge critical path.
Install only required dependencies. Reuse builds and fixtures when their inputs
match. Compare cache hit rate and restore/save cost against rebuilding; a cache
can cost more than the work it replaces. Artifact caching and passing-test
evidence are separate: apply the parent's freshness rule to the latter.
Inspect test duration distribution before adding shards. Split disproportionate
work units where the runner can actually schedule them, then measure both wall
time and total compute. Do not assume that doubling workers halves runtime.
## Bound concurrency and preserve isolation
Separate parallel investigation from parallel test execution. Independent failure
analysis can shorten diagnosis while a full OS matrix is already parallel.
Use the ownership and handoff procedure in [local feedback](local-feedback.md)
when splitting investigations. Parallelize work that can advance independently;
serialize edits to shared files and integrate before broad validation. Measure
diagnosis time, avoided reruns, and final required execution separately. Do not
claim that more investigators shorten a mandatory check's own runtime.
Account for other agents and worktrees on the same machine before starting heavy
suites. Use an existing coordinator or lock when available; deduplicate identical
in-flight inputs only when the result and ownership can be observed. Allow cheap
focused tests to proceed within capacity. Do not cancel another worker's run or
reclaim its lock without ownership evidence.
Inspect resource contention before extending timeouts. Adding a scheduler, cache,
or lock has its own correctness costs: invalidation, stale ownership, cancellation,
and partial results. Do not implement these as incidental fixes to a test failure.
State sharing requires explicit eligibility and cleanup. Keep isolation for tests
with timers, global mutations, or other state that cannot be safely reset. Schema
snapshots can accelerate ordinary DB tests; retain real migration-path checks.
Prebuilt environments must track toolchain and dependency changes. Reduced setup
must not silently reduce platform coverage or hide fixture leakage.
## Speed up a slow test suite
Use when the suite itself is the bottleneck. Never trade correctness for speed:
keep every check, and let the repository's required gate (CI or the merge gate)
run the complete suite. Local runs follow the affected-check scope in Phase 1 of
SKILL.md; [test selection](test-selection.md) covers selection tools. Change
tests only within the requested scope and report each change. Numbers behind
these rules: [test evidence](test-evidence.md).
Do not hide a symptom: no longer timeouts, added retries, forced exits
(`--forceExit`, `--exit`, `--test-force-exit`, `os._exit`), disabled isolation,
skipped tests, or weakened assertions. Each hides a cause; find the cause.
1. Baseline. Find the exact command the gate runs (CI configuration, package
scripts, Makefile, AGENTS.md) and how to run one file and one test; do not guess
a runner. Run it once and record wall time, passed, failed, and skipped counts,
worker count, CPU count (`nproc` or `sysctl -n hw.ncpu`), and the time of each
part.
2. Profile each unit (file, package, or suite):
`node scripts/test_profile.js --cmd "<run one unit with {file}>" UNITS...`.
Read four signals: WAITING units (low CPU share, high wall time); the serial
sum against wall time at the current worker count; the long pole against the
projected wall time; and time before the first test or after the last one,
which is serial setup. High CPU spent again on unchanged inputs is repeated
work; the rest is real work. A unit that fails alone is order dependent; see
[flaky tests](flaky-tests.md). `node scripts/test_smells.js TEST_DIRS` adds
static hints: sleeps, fixed ports and paths, real network calls, retry
settings, serial-only settings, and focused tests.
3. Remove waits first; they are usually the largest share. Parallelism before
this only overlaps the waiting, and the idle unit stays the long pole. Diagnose
and fix with [test waits](test-waits.md). Re-profile after each fix; stop when
no unit is WAITING or each remaining one has a stated reason.
4. Remove repeated work. Reuse a result only when every input that decides it is
hashed and the hash is recomputed where the result is reused. A variant run that
changes a document or script must rerun every check that reads it. Variant
runs such as mutation or deletion copies are the usual source of repetition.
5. Parallelize after isolation: [test parallelism](test-parallelism.md) has the
isolation checklist, worker settings per runner, and proof runs. Three
consecutive green runs and one random-order run are required; a failure only in
parallel is an isolation bug, not a reason to lower workers.
6. Split the long pole. With P workers, wall time is at least
max(longest unit, total / P). Split the longest unit by feature or move its slow
part into its own unit, and start the slowest units first.
7. Cut serial setup and cache. Key dependency caches by lockfile, start services
once per worker, and reuse compiled output. Cache test results by input hash
only for hermetic tests (Bazel, the Go test cache, Nx, Turborepo), and do not
bypass such a cache by habit. In CI, fix overhead before the tests themselves:
cache the environment, skip unaffected steps, finish early, and never retry
blindly. [Test parallelism](test-parallelism.md) has settings per ecosystem.
8. Order the local run: tests that failed recently first, then new or changed
tests, then the fastest.
9. Prove equivalence with the same command on the same machine. Passing checks
must not decrease and no skips are added. Shared CI runners vary between runs;
compare unchanged units before attributing a CI difference to the change. When
a slow test becomes fast, confirm it still fails for the defect it guards; slow
tests sometimes pass for the wrong reason or with a weaker assertion than they
could have.
10. Lock it in. Add a regression test for each structural fix, such as a fixture
that leaves long timers behind and must still exit within a few seconds.
Budget new and changed test files:
`node scripts/test_profile.js --cmd "<run one file>" --max-seconds 2 --fail-on-waiting --repeat 5 FILES`.
The tdd skill carries the test design checklist; the setup skill carries
templates for writing the rules where contributors and agents read them.
A speed-up is done when:
- before and after numbers come from the same command, per part and in total;
- the same or more checks pass, with no new skips and no weakened assertions;
- the complete suite passes three times in a row at the new worker count, and
once in random order where the runner supports it;
- no unit is WAITING, or each remaining one is explained;
- structural fixes have regression tests, the rules are written down, and the
required gate runs everything.
When the remaining time is real work on inputs that change, further gains are
structural: sharding or larger runners. They usually cost more aggregate compute,
so present them as options with their cost instead of applying them.
## Record the result
Compare the same checks before and after an optimization and verify behavior at
the affected boundaries. Record latency, aggregate compute when available, setup
cost, and correctness evidence. Update the owning agent guidance when new tests
must follow eligibility or cleanup rules. Structural simplification alone does
not establish measured savings.
Report p95 as well as the median; uneven times cause more context switching than
slow but predictable ones. Show an expected duration for CI where the platform
allows it. Count compute, money, and energy with the time saved: disable idle
scheduled workflows and cut redundant runs.
These principles draw on [Linear's CI redesign](https://linear.app/now/ci-bottleneck-reworked).
Its numerical results are examples, not targets or evidence for another project.
